21.9  sort, uniq and cut

TL;DR
  - sort sorts lines: -n by number, -h by size with units (2K, 1G), -r in
    reverse, -k 2 by the second column.
  - uniq removes repeated lines that follow each other, so sort first. uniq -c
    counts them.
  - cut keeps some columns: -d, sets the separator, -f 1,3 the columns.

      sort ips.txt | uniq -c | sort -rn | head    # most frequent lines
      du -sh * | sort -h                          # biggest items last
      cut -d: -f1,7 /etc/passwd                   # users and their shells

Pitfalls
  - uniq without sort only merges neighbours: an unsorted file keeps its
    duplicates.
  - sort without -n compares text, so 10 comes before 9.
  - cut splits on one character: with columns separated by several spaces, use
    awk (see 21.3).
  - The order depends on your language settings (case, accents). LC_ALL=C sort
    sorts by byte value, and faster.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 21.9 --why

Related
  Commands  sort, uniq, cut, wc
  See also  idk 21.3 awk
